  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "In the dining room, guests walk out after seeing a five-thousand-ruble pigeon dish on the menu, while both Denis and Nikita separately plot to win Katya back at tonight's staff party.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Филе дикого голубя с конфетой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А почему так дорого?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Наверное, он очень редкий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нормальный ресторан поблизости есть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что нового, Денис?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С Катей не могу помириться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Цветы выбрасывает, трубку не берёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты издеваешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Сегодня вечером все выпьют и расслабятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ещё раз попробую поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Слушай, ты не мог бы с Катей поговорить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Я мучаюсь и помириться хочу.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Филе дикого голубя с конфетой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fillet of wild pigeon with a bonbon.' The grammar packs 'fillet' and 'sweet' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange. Denis answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А почему так дорого?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And why is it so expensive?' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'and' before landing on 'expensive'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Наверное, он очень редкий.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Probably, it is very rare.' In Russian, the idea of 'probably' flows naturally into 'rare' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Нормальный ресторан поблизости есть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Is there a normal restaurant nearby?' Denis raises the pitch on 'there is' to turn the phrase into a direct spoken question. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Что нового, Денис?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What is new, Denis?' Kostya brings out the contrast between 'what' and 'new' in a few short words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С Катей не могу помириться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I cannot make up with Katya.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'with' and finishing on 'to make peace'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Цветы выбрасывает, трубку не берёт.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She throws away flowers and does not pick up the phone.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'flowers' and finishing on 'takes'. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Ты издеваешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you kidding?' Notice how Russian expresses 'you' and 'mock' with crisp inflection rather than helper words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kostya",
      "t": "Сегодня вечером все выпьют и расслабятся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Tonight everyone will have a drink and relax.' The grammar packs 'tonight' and 'will relax' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ещё раз попробую поговорить.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will try talking to her once more.' The meaning hinges on 'else' working together with 'to talk' in spoken Russian. Nikita answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Слушай, ты не мог бы с Катей поговорить?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Listen, could you talk to Katya?' The grammar packs 'listen' and 'to talk' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nikita",
      "t": "Я мучаюсь и помириться хочу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am agonizing and want to make up.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'I' and finishing on 'want'. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
